Package: simFastBOIN
Type: Package
Title: Fast Simulation of Bayesian Optimal Interval Designs for Phase I and
        Phase I/II Trials
Version: 2.2.0.9000
Date: 2026-11-22
Authors@R: person(given = "Gosuke",
                  family = "Homma",
                  role = c("aut", "cre"),
                  email = "my.name.is.gosuke@gmail.com",
                  comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-6854-5627"))
Description: Design and evaluate phase I dose-finding trials that use the
    Bayesian optimal interval (BOIN) design of Liu and Yuan (2015)
    <doi:10.1111/rssc.12089>. Functions are provided to tabulate the decision
    boundaries, to simulate trials, to estimate the dose-toxicity curve under a
    monotonicity constraint and to select the maximum tolerated dose. The
    simulation engine is written in C++ and draws one random variate per
    patient in enrollment order, which reproduces the reference implementation
    in the 'BOIN' package trial by trial for a given seed. The traditional 3+3
    design is provided as a comparator, with operating characteristics obtained
    in closed form rather than by simulation. Prior information on the
    toxicity of the doses enters the design through the iBOIN boundaries of
    Zhou and colleagues (2021) <doi:10.1002/pst.2121>, and toxicity grades
    through the gBOIN design of Mu and colleagues (2019)
    <doi:10.1111/rssc.12263>. For toxicity that is observed
    late, the time-to-event BOIN design is provided with the two estimators of
    Yuan and colleagues (2018) <doi:10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-18-0246> and Lin and
    Yuan (2020) <doi:10.1093/biostatistics/kxz007>. Patients who arrive while
    the dose escalation waits can be backfilled to lower doses with the designs
    of Zhao and colleagues (2024) <doi:10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-23-2585> and Chen
    and colleagues (2026) <doi:10.1007/s43441-026-00994-0>, the latter with
    the suspension rules of Chen and colleagues (2025)
    <doi:10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-24-3669>. Trials of two drugs given together
    are handled by the drug-combination design of Lin and Yin (2017)
    <doi:10.1177/0962280215594494>. The optimal biological dose, which
    weighs the response against the toxicity, is found with the BOIN-ET design
    of Takeda and colleagues (2018) <doi:10.1002/pst.1864>, with the estimators
    and selection rules of the 'boinet' package, its time-to-event version
    (Takeda and colleagues, 2020) <doi:10.1002/pst.1995>, its backfill
    version (Takeda and colleagues, 2025) <doi:10.1002/pst.2470> and its
    version for graded toxicity and efficacy, gBOIN-ET (Takeda and
    colleagues, 2022) <doi:10.1002/bimj.202100263>, and with
    the BOIN12 design of Lin and colleagues (2020) <doi:10.1200/PO.20.00257>,
    which weighs them by a utility, and its time-to-event version (Zhou and
    colleagues, 2022) <doi:10.1002/sim.9337>. For two drugs given together,
    the optimal dose combination is found with the Comb-BOIN12 design of Lu
    and colleagues (2025) <doi:10.1080/19466315.2024.2370403>, also in its
    time-to-event version, and the BOIN-ETC design of Kakizume and colleagues
    (2024) <doi:10.1177/09622802241236936>. The titration of the BOIN design
    can be limited to the lowest doses, as in the accelerated design of Kojima
    and colleagues (2024) <doi:10.1007/s43441-024-00692-9>. The operating
    characteristics of several designs can be tabulated and plotted side by
    side.
